feat(db2): add seven two-string operations, and make the replay able to fail

Seven operations taking two bounded strings move onto the bus:
learning.artifact_flag_review, learning.verdict_suppressed,
learning.curator_invalidate_doc, maintenance.css_migration_assert_conventions,
organization.doc_assets_delete_for_doc, organization.ontology_map and
index.minhash_delete_file.

Reviewing artifact_flag_review against the real database turned up two defects
and then a third that had been hiding both.

The operation could never have worked. Its payload merge was a CASE expression
whose first arm compared payload against '', and payload is JSONB on Postgres,
so the server raised 22P02 while planning -- for a matching row and a missing
one alike. Removing that arm was not enough: both arms ended in ::text, and
Postgres will not assign text to a jsonb column. The merge now happens in C
with cJSON, which needs no JSON SQL and behaves the same on both backends, and
the write casts its bound parameter with ::jsonb. Reading the row first also
means a missing artifact is refused rather than acknowledged, which the single
UPDATE could not do. The SQLite shim learned to drop ::jsonb the way it already
drops ::text.

Nothing caught this because nothing could. The replay's module binary is built
from a source closure the exporter decides, so make cannot see what it depends
on: the production target had no prerequisites at all and the replay target had
only its own main. Once built, neither was ever rebuilt. Every replay run since
the binary was first produced tested whichever DB2 sources happened to be in
the tree that day -- including the runs this branch has been reporting as
verification. Both targets are phony now, so each invocation re-exports and
rebuilds.

With that fixed the replay can be made to prove something. It applies the
schema itself before starting the module and seeds one committed artifact
carrying a payload of its own, because on a fresh schema every read answers
zero and every write against a missing row is refused, and neither
distinguishes a working operation from a broken one. The replay flags that
artifact over the bus, and the script then reads the row back and fails unless
the artifact's own key survived beside the flag and the state was reset. The
other six operations are replayed too, against what a fresh schema can offer.

Of those six, three carry findings. verdict_suppressed answers "not suppressed"
on every failure path, so a caller using it as a gate lets suppressed content
through whenever the database is unreachable. curator_invalidate_doc reads at
most 1024 of a document's chunks and stops, leaving the artifacts citing later
chunks valid while reporting a count that looks complete.
css_migration_assert_conventions reads two configuration flags -- which over
the bus are the module process's, not the caller's -- and answers zero when
either is off, the same zero as a project with nothing indexed; it also writes
the caller's timestamp rather than DB2's clock.

Verified: both DB2 unit suites, go test -count=1 ./db2/, the whole scripts/tests
suite, 61 lint checks, and the process replay against Postgres on 192.168.1.252
with a module rebuilt from this tree.
